        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This review summarizes mechanisms by which microbial metabolites influence immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) efficacy. For inosine, it reports that Bifidobacterium pseudolongum produces inosine which translocates systemically upon gut barrier dysfunction to activate antitumor T cells and potentiate ICI efficacy, citing Mager and colleagues [112]. For short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), it describes a prospective study of 52 patients with solid tumors where elevated fecal levels of acetate, propionate, butyrate, and valerate were associated with prolonged progression-free survival and better response to anti-PD-1 therapy [111, 156], and notes consistent findings in non-small cell lung cancer patients [157]. It details mechanisms such as SCFA-mediated HDAC inhibition and GPR signaling to enhance CD8+ T cell function. It also provides a quantitative example for a different metabolite, noting that low serum L-arginine concentration (less than 42 μM) was associated with poor ICI outcomes in a multicohort study [167], illustrating the type of data available for metabolite biomarkers.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The text lists clinical trials of fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) and probiotics in cancer immunotherapy (Table 1, Table 2). It discusses other metabolites like tryptophan derivatives, TMAO, and vitamin B5 in relation to ICI response. It covers microbial signatures associated with immune-related adverse events (irAEs) and strategies to mitigate them. It also describes multi-kingdom biomarkers including fungi and viruses, and emerging technologies like engineered bacteria and bacteriophage therapy.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied text does not provide the specific quantitative physiological concentrations (e.g., μM, ng/mL) of inosine or SCFAs measured inside the human tumour microenvironment or serum that correlate with ICI response. 